# Governed SonarQube Cloud Remediation — C#/.NET and Gemini

**Document status:** Generic implementation specification and coding-agent handoff.  
**Audience:** Coding agents, software engineers, and security reviewers.  
**Publication note:** This public template contains no real organization configuration, proprietary source, credentials, or private repository references. Keep actual environment details only in approved private systems.

> **Instruction to the coding agent:** Treat this document as the implementation contract. Work incrementally, demonstrate each milestone, and never invent test, scanner, or security validation results. First inspect the actual repository, tooling, and approved integrations. Where the document says “verify,” inspect official API/SDK documentation and the organization's configuration before coding against it. Do not independently upload company source code or secrets to tools that have not been approved.

## 1. Goal, context, and non-goals

Build a **developer-triggered C#/.NET CLI** that selects an existing **SonarQube Cloud** finding in a **local C# repository**, extracts a minimal source-code context with **Roslyn**, requests tests and a minimal fix from **Gemini via the company's approved Google Cloud Vertex AI endpoint**, and validates the change through **configurable, risk-based quality gates**. A developer must review and approve the result. The same core engine should later support CI/CD orchestration.

**Differentiator:** This is not merely “send finding to LLM, apply patch.” The framework must apply a **test-first process**, enforce **scope and security policies**, perform **independent build/test/scanner checks**, classify **issue risk and actual patch risk separately**, and produce an **auditable evidence report**.

**Assumed scope:** implementation in C#; SonarQube **Cloud**, not Server; Gemini through an authorized **Vertex AI API**; a C#/.NET repository (which may contain multiple projects); initial interface is a local console tool, not an automatic merge bot.

**Not in MVP:** autonomous commits/pushes/merges; modification of Sonar rules to make a finding disappear; mass-fixing an issue backlog; full repository upload to Gemini; guaranteed runtime dependency discovery; a custom AI agent/MCP layer unless it demonstrably simplifies a specific requirement. Use the SonarQube Cloud REST API initially, not MCP.

## 2. Critical decisions and prerequisites to resolve *inside the company*

Before writing an external integration, obtain or inspect the following. Ask a concise question only when the repository and environment do not answer it:

| Area | Required facts / decision |
|---|---|
| Repository | .NET SDK/runtime and target framework, `.sln`/`.csproj`, test framework and commands, build environment, Git branch policy |
| SonarQube Cloud | **US or EU instance**; organization key and project key; issue/branch permissions; whether CI or automatic analysis is currently configured; branch/PR analysis availability |
| Vertex AI | Approved Google Cloud project, location, Gemini model ID, IAM/ADC authentication method, input logging/retention and source-code classification approval |
| Controls | Approved execution environment; whether local scanner uploads and temporary branch/PR analysis are permitted; security tools; who may approve remediation |

**Sonar API endpoints are region-specific.** V1 is `https://sonarcloud.io/api` for the EU instance and `https://sonarqube.us/api` for the US instance. The V2 APIs are evolving, so use the live instance's API documentation for the specific endpoints and parameters. Use bearer token authentication, project-scoped permissions, pagination, and rate-limit handling. A read-only issue lookup does **not** perform a new Sonar analysis.

**Vertex AI SDK:** First evaluate the current official `Google.GenAI` .NET package, which supports Vertex/Google Cloud authentication. Configure the SDK for **Vertex AI / enterprise service**, not Gemini Developer API with a personal API key. Use an organization-approved identity (prefer Application Default Credentials / workload identity) and explicit project, region, and model configuration. Do not embed service-account keys in code or JSON files. Use the version/initialization recommended by the SDK actually available in the company.

**Source-code permission:** An approved Gemini endpoint is not automatic permission to transmit all proprietary source. Enforce an allowlist for permissible file paths/data classes, send only minimal snippets, redact secrets, and avoid logging prompts or responses that contain proprietary code unless explicitly authorized.

